  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q24-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "24",
    "prompt": "Prove that\n$$(\\sin\\theta+\\csc\\theta)^2+(\\cos\\theta+\\sec\\theta)^2\\ge 9.$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The expression equals $5+\\dfrac{4}{\\sin^2 2\\theta}\\ge9$.\n\nExpand the squares, use the reciprocal and Pythagorean identities, then apply $\\sin2\\theta=2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta$ and $0<\\sin^22\\theta\\le1$.",
      "worked": "Let $E=(\\sin\\theta+\\csc\\theta)^2+(\\cos\\theta+\\sec\\theta)^2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}E&=(\\sin^2\\theta+2+\\csc^2\\theta)+(\\cos^2\\theta+2+\\sec^2\\theta)\\\\E&=5+\\csc^2\\theta+\\sec^2\\theta\\\\E&=5+\\frac{1}{\\sin^2\\theta}+\\frac{1}{\\cos^2\\theta}\\\\E&=5+\\frac{\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta}{\\sin^2\\theta\\cos^2\\theta}\\\\E&=5+\\frac{1}{\\sin^2\\theta\\cos^2\\theta}\\\\E&=5+\\frac{4}{(2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta)^2}\\\\E&=5+\\frac{4}{\\sin^22\\theta}.\\end{align*}$$\nWhere the original expression is defined, $0<\\sin^22\\theta\\le1$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}E&\\ge5+4\\\\E&\\ge9.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 100
  },
